.TH CJSON_PARSE_MP 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cjson_parse_mp, cjson_parse \- parse a null-terminated JSON string into a DOM tree
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cjson.h>

cjson cjson_parse_mp(const char *json_str, char **err_str,
                     ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);
/* convenience wrapper; default allocator */
static inline cjson cjson_parse(const char *json_str,
                                char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Parses json_str using a recursive-descent parser and returns the root
node of the resulting DOM tree.  All seven JSON value kinds are
supported: null, boolean, integer (stored as long long), number (double),
string (UTF-8, owned heap copy), array (cvec of child cjson), and object
(chmap of char*->cjson).

Integer literals with no decimal point or exponent are stored as
CJSON_INTEGER; all others (including integers that overflow long long)
as CJSON_FLOAT.  A number literal's decimal-text length is not limited;
numbers that overflow IEEE 754 double (i.e. strtod returns infinity)
produce a parse error.

String escape sequences \e", \e\e, \e/, \eb, \ef, \en, \er, \et, and
\euXXXX (including surrogate pairs) are decoded to UTF-8.  The
\eu0000 is explicitly rejected with a parse error: cjson
stores strings as null-terminated char * values and cannot
represent embedded null bytes.

A document nested more than 500 levels deep (arrays and/or objects,
in any combination) is rejected with a parse error rather than
recursing further; this bounds worst-case parse-time stack usage
against a pathologically or maliciously deeply-nested document.
Ordinary JSON documents never approach this depth.

cjson_parse() is a static inline wrapper that calls
cjson_parse_mp(json_str, err_str, NULL).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
json_str  Null-terminated JSON text.
err_str   Out-parameter for the parse error message.  On success
          *err_str is set to NULL.  On failure a heap-allocated
          null-terminated string, allocated through mp, is stored in
          *err_str; the caller must free it with
          cjson_serialize_free_mp() (passing the same mp) when done.
          Pass NULL to discard the error message.
mp        Custom memory management procedures for all nodes in the
          resulting tree, or NULL for the default allocator.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Root cjson node on success.
NULL on parse failure; if err_str was non-NULL, *err_str holds the
reason (caller must free it with cjson_serialize_free_mp(), passing
the same mp).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The caller owns the returned tree and must free it with cjson_destroy().
The error string (when requested) is allocated through the same mp
used for the parse; free it with cjson_serialize_free_mp(err, mp)
(or cjson_serialize_free(err) for the default allocator).

Number parsing always treats '.' as the decimal separator, per RFC 8259,
regardless of the calling thread's ambient LC_NUMERIC locale setting.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
char *err = NULL;
cjson doc = cjson_parse_mp(input, &err, NULL);
if (!doc) {
    fprintf(stderr, "parse failed: %s\en", err);
    cjson_serialize_free(err);
    return -1;
}
/* use doc ... */
cjson_destroy(doc);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_parse_n_mp (3),
.BR cjson_destroy (3),
.BR cjson_serialize (3),
.BR cjson_serialize_free_mp (3)
